  "notes": [],
  "dividers": [],
  "blocks": [],
  "meta": {
    "title": "Every request is eventually granted",
    "blurb": "G(r → F g), the response property of model checking. Letters are r (request), g (grant) and n (nothing). A run is stuck in “pending” forever exactly when some request is never answered — so “idle” must recur.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "(n)",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "nothing ever asked"
      },
      {
        "w": "(rg)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "r(nng)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "rrg(n)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "r(n)",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "waits forever"
      },
      {
        "w": "(rn)",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "g(r)",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "ltl",
        "model-checking",
        "liveness"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "A **liveness** property: something good eventually happens. No finite prefix can violate it — however long a request has been waiting, a grant might still come — which is why it needs an ω-automaton rather than a finite one, and why a finite test run can never refute it.\n\nTwo states suffice. \"pending\" means an unanswered request exists; several requests before a grant are served by that one grant, which matches the LTL reading. The word is accepted when \"idle\" is visited infinitely often. Model checkers such as SPIN check a system against a property like this by building the product of the system with an automaton for the property's *negation* and searching for an accepting lasso."
    }
  }
}
